s) fail — CIVO is forward-pass representational damage\n- C8: TCN (Temporal Conditioning Noise) — forward-pass noise injection into dt_embedding during imagination — prevents CIVO (Walker σ=0.2, NVE&lt;1.0×, 90% baseline) and reduces it in Cheetah (σ=0.5, NVE from 8-108× to 1.5-4×). Dose-response relationship observed.\n\nExperiments span 4 DMC environments (Walker Walk, Cheetah Run, Cartpole Swingup, Hopper Hop), 30+ configurations, 60+ seeds.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe paper's imagined experiment list includes:\n1. Walker Walk 008d (trained dt_emb, collapse) — 2 seeds, both NVE>1.5×\n2. Walker Walk 008f (dt_embed_dim=0, stable) — 4+ seeds, all NVE<1.5×\n3. Walker Walk 008e_v3 (mitigation, dt=1 imag) — 4 seeds, 2/4 collapse (Pathway B)\n4. Walker Walk 008a (untrained dt_emb) — 3 seeds, all NVE<1.0×\n5. Walker Walk FROZEN dt_emb — 1 seed, gradient starvation\n6. Cheetah Run baseline — 5 seeds\n7. Cheetah Run trained dt_emb — 3 seeds, catastrophic CIVO (NVE 8-70×)\n8. Cheetah Run dt_embed_dim=0 — 2+ seeds, no CIVO\n9. Cheetah Run untrained dt_emb — 2+ seeds, value suppression\n10. RSSM prediction quality analysis (dt=1 vs dt=2)\n11. Actor entropy analysis\n12. NVE threshold sensitivity analysis (AUROC=0.969)\n13. Cartpole Swingup baseline — 3 seeds\n14. Cartpole Swingup trained dt_emb — 3 seeds, 3/3 NVE>1.5×\n15. Cartpole Swingup dt_embed_dim=0 — 3 seeds, 0/3 CIVO\n16. Hopper Hop validation — 3 seeds × 3 configs (P2 stretch goal)\n17. Uncertainty-gated imagination truncation pilot\n18. Value prediction distribution analysis (P2)\n19. dt_embedding cosine similarity analysis (P2)\n\nThe paper claims 4 environments, 30+ configs, 60+ seeds. Many of the P2 items (Hopper, Cartpole, cosine similarity) are listed as imagined/predicted in the context — check if the paper actually presents real data or imagined data for these.\n\n## Your Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nEvaluate the following dimensions thoroughly:\n\n### 1. Methodological Rigor\n- Is the factorial experiment design sound? Are the controls adequate?\n- Is the NVE metric well-defined and meaningful? Any issues with the normalization?\n- Is the causal reasoning valid (necessary/sufficient conditions for CIVO)?\n- Are there confounds that the authors haven't addressed?\n\n### 2. Experimental Coverage and Validity\n- Do the experiments adequately support all 8 claims?\n- Are seed counts sufficient for statistical conclusions? (Note: many Walker experiments have only 2 seeds)\n- Is there selection bias in which results are reported?\n- The paper claims \"60+ seeds\" — verify if this is plausible given the reported experiments\n- Are the Cartpole and Hopper results real or extrapolated? (The review_context marks them as imagined experiments with predicted results)\n- Are error bars, confidence intervals, or statistical tests reported?\n\n### 3. Reproducibility\n- Are hyperparameters and implementation details sufficient for reproduction?\n- Is the code available or described in enough detail?\n\n### 4. TCN as Mitigation\n- Is TCN (noise injection) a principled solution or a hack?\n- The dose-response has only 2 data points (σ=0.2 for Walker, σ=0.5 for Cheetah) — is this sufficient?\n- TCN σ=0.5 on Cheetah only reduces NVE to 1.5-4× (still above the 1.5× threshold) — is this really \"mitigation\"?\n- No adaptive mechanism proposed — how practical is manual σ tuning?\n\n### 5. CoRL Relevance\n- CoRL focuses on robot learning. This paper studies DreamerV3 on DMC benchmark tasks. Is this sufficiently relevant to robotics?\n- No real-robot experiments or discussion of real-world applicability.\n\n## Output Format\n\nProvide your review in this exact structure:\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentence summary of the paper from your perspective]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list, each with a short bold title and 1-2 sentence explanation]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list, each with a short bold title and 1-2 